{% raw %}<section class="band band-photo band-photo-text"><img src="/images/2018/06/Cabinetry_Header-hero.webp" alt="All Wood Cabinets" width="1200" height="900" fetchpriority="high" class="band-img"><div class="wrap"><div class="band-card"><h1>Kitchen Renovation ROI</h1></div></div></section><section class="band"><div class="wrap"><h2>Is There a Return on Getting the Kitchen You <br>Actually Want?</h2><div class="el-text"><p>Homeowners ask us this all the time, usually somewhere between picking out cabinets and wondering if they're about to overspend: Is this actually worth it? Not the safest, most resale-friendly version of a kitchen, but the one they actually want. So we've been paying close attention to it: renovation trends, homeowner satisfaction data, resale patterns, alongside what we hear directly from homeowners across Metro New Orleans and the Northshore</p></div><div class="el-text"><p>Here's what we're seeing and what it means if you're renovating a kitchen in New Orleans, Mandeville or anywhere in between.</p></div></div></section><section class="band"><div class="wrap"><div class="el-row" style="--cols:50fr 50fr"><div class="el-col"><h2>What Return Should You Actually Be Looking For?</h2><div class="el-text"><p>Most of what gets written about kitchen renovation return on investment (ROI) is really answering one specific question: what will you get back if you sell your house? That's a fair question. But it's the wrong one for most homeowners, because most people renovating a kitchen aren't selling next year.</p></div><div class="el-text"><p>If you plan to stay in your home for years to come, the return that matters most isn't measured by resale value. It's the everyday return. The enjoyment you get from a kitchen that's easier to cook in, more comfortable to gather in and a pleasure to walk into every day.</p></div><div class="el-text"><p>That distinction shapes how we think about this question and how we think about “the kitchen you want” in the first place. So we're going to look at both sides.</p></div></div><div class="el-col"><figure class="el-img"><img src="/images/2024/10/10b.webp" alt="Open plan kitchen with a long island and large windows" width="1200" height="800" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></figure></div></div></div></section><section class="band"><div class="wrap"><div class="el-row" style="--cols:50fr 50fr"><div class="el-col"><figure class="el-img"><img src="/images/2026/07/satisfaction-1-1.webp" alt="Kitchen renovations score 10/10 for homeowner satisfaction" width="1200" height="1200" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></figure></div><div class="el-col"><h2>The Return You Get From a Kitchen You Actually Enjoy</h2><div class="el-text"><p>Here's one of the most interesting findings: kitchen remodels consistently rank among the highest home improvement projects for homeowner satisfaction. After renovating their kitchen, many homeowners say they spend more time at home, enjoy entertaining more and simply enjoy their homes more every day.</p></div><div class="el-text"><p>That's the kind of return that comes from designing a kitchen around the way you really live, not the return you'll see on a closing statement, but the one you'll experience every single day.</p></div><div class="el-text"><p>None of that shows up on a closing statement. But if you plan to enjoy your kitchen for years to come, not sell your home next spring, that may be the return that matters most.</p></div></div></div></div></section><section class="band"><div class="wrap"><h2>Does Getting What You Want Still Make Financial Sense?</h2><div class="el-text"><p>Yes and it's a stronger case than most homeowners realize. A thoughtfully planned kitchen remodel with new cabinets, updated countertops and a layout designed around the way you actually cook and live, often returns most of its cost and sometimes even more. Whether it's a complete renovation or a more focused update, the key is making decisions that fit the way you'll use the space.</p></div><div class="el-text"><p>Of course, not every dollar spent adds the same value. Overspending beyond what your home can reasonably support or investing heavily in features that don't match your neighborhood, can reduce your financial return. But the heart of a well-designed kitchen: a functional layout, quality cabinetry and durable materials is almost always a worthwhile investment.</p></div></div></section><section class="band"><div class="wrap"><h2>What This Means for Metro New Orleans and <br>Northshore Homes</h2><div class="el-text"><p>A lot of what informs this is national data, so we want to be straightforward about that. But some of it lines up especially well with what we see in our own showrooms located in New Orleans, in Mandeville, and everywhere in between.</p></div><div class="el-text"><p>Homes across Metro New Orleans and the Northshore skew older: shotguns, doubles, century-old layouts, homes that have been added onto and adjusted over generations. Kitchens in these homes are often the one room that hasn't kept pace with how the rest of the house is used. And hosting isn't optional here. Families gather in the kitchen. A kitchen that doesn't work for that isn't just a resale liability, it's something you deal with every single time you have people over.</p></div></div></section><section class="band"><div class="wrap"><h2>So, Is There a Return on the Kitchen You Actually Want?</h2><div class="el-text"><p>Based on what we're seeing, the honest answer is yes. If the question is “will I get my money back if I sell,” a well-planned kitchen remodel is one of the better-recouping projects you can take on, whether it's a full renovation or a smaller refresh. If the question is “will a kitchen built around what I actually want make my home work better for the years I'm living in it,” the answer is just as strong and it's the return most homeowners actually care about.</p></div><div class="el-text"><p>That's really the conversation we want to have with you. Not how much you should spend, but what you actually need your kitchen to do for you.</p></div><div class="el-block"><p class="btn-row"><a class="btn" href="/request-consultation/">Let&#x27;s Talk About YOUR Kitchen.</a></p></div></div></section>{% endraw %}
